Imagine you are sitting on the steps of a temple tank in Madurai. The steps go down in neat rows, the tiles on the floor repeat in squares, and somewhere a bell rings every few seconds. Your brain quietly notices all of this: *things repeat, things grow in a regular way.* That noticing is where mathematics begins.

Many people think maths is only about sums and answers. But mathematicians describe their subject in a different way: **mathematics is the search for patterns, and for explanations of why those patterns happen.**

Patterns are everywhere:

- the phases of the Moon repeat about every 29½ days;
- the monsoon arrives in Kerala around the start of June, year after year;
- a sunflower packs its seeds in beautiful spirals;
- the numbers on a clock come back to 12 again and again.

Once we notice a pattern, we can *predict*. Farmers predict the rains, astronomers predict eclipses, engineers predict how a bridge will bend. Prediction is one of the most useful powers humans have, and it grows out of pattern-spotting.

Mathematics also has a playful side. Some patterns are studied simply because they are beautiful. Often, centuries later, a 'useless' pattern turns out to run a computer, a phone network or a satellite. So in this chapter we will play with patterns in **numbers** and patterns in **shapes**, and then discover that the two are secretly linked.

> **Did you know?** Indian mathematicians such as Pingala (around 2,000 years ago) studied patterns in the rhythms of Sanskrit poetry — and found number patterns that are still studied today.

Akshi runs around the school ground every morning. Her friend Toshi runs around the smaller garden next to it — three times. Who runs more? To answer, we need the **distance around** each shape. That distance is the **perimeter**.

**Perimeter = the total length of the boundary of a closed shape.** It is a length, so it is measured in mm, cm, m or km.

For any polygon, just **add all the sides**. A triangle with sides 5 cm, 6 cm and 7 cm has perimeter 5 + 6 + 7 = 18 cm.

Where is perimeter used?

- fencing a field or a garden;
- putting lace or a border around a tablecloth;
- running laps around a track;
- framing a photo.

Suppose the ground is a rectangle 150 m by 80 m: once around is 150 + 80 + 150 + 80 = 460 m. Toshi's garden is 60 m by 45 m: once round is 210 m, three times is 630 m. Toshi runs more!

Stand in front of the Taj Mahal in Agra and look straight at it. The left half is a mirror copy of the right half — domes, minarets, arches, even the long pool in front. Your eye feels calm and balanced. That feeling has a mathematical name: **symmetry**.

Symmetry appears everywhere:

- in **nature** — butterfly wings, leaves, flowers, snowflakes, starfish, our faces (almost!);
- in **art** — rangoli, kolam, block-print fabrics, Madhubani borders;
- in **architecture** — temples, gurdwaras, mosques, the India Gate;
- in **letters and logos** — A, H, M, O, X; the wheel in our national flag.

There are two main kinds you will learn in this chapter:

1. **Line (reflection) symmetry** — a figure can be folded so the halves match.
2. **Rotational symmetry** — a figure looks the same after being turned partway round.

> **Did you know?** The Ashoka Chakra on the Indian flag has 24 spokes, so it looks exactly the same after a turn of just 15°.

It is a June afternoon in Kolkata. The first monsoon storm has just passed. You step outside and notice: the air smells different, earthworms have appeared on the path, a puddle shines on the road, and by evening the puddle has almost gone. Where did the water go? Why did the worms come up? Why does rain smell like that?

If you have ever wondered things like this, congratulations — **you are already thinking like a scientist.**

**Science** is a way of understanding the world around us by asking questions, observing carefully, testing ideas, and learning from evidence. It is not just a subject in a textbook; it is a habit of mind.

Science studies everything:

- the tiniest things — cells, atoms, germs;
- the biggest — stars, galaxies, the whole universe;
- the living — plants, animals, our own bodies;
- the non-living — rocks, water, air, electricity, magnets;
- and how they all connect, from the food we eat to the climate of our planet.

This year's science book is called *Curiosity*, and that is exactly the point: curiosity is the engine of science.

> **Did you know?** The smell after the first rain has a name, *petrichor*. Part of it comes from oils and from a substance released by tiny soil bacteria. Kannauj in Uttar Pradesh even makes a traditional 'mitti attar' that captures this earthy scent.

This chapter is a doorway to a year of exploring. In your science book you will investigate:

- **food** — where it comes from, its nutrients, and a balanced diet;
- **materials** — what things are made of and how to sort them;
- **plants and animals** — their diversity and how they suit their homes;
- **magnets** — attraction, repulsion and the compass;
- **measurement** — lengths, movement and units;
- **temperature** — hot and cold, thermometers;
- **water** — states of matter, evaporation and condensation;
- **living and non-living** things — what makes something alive;
- **nature's treasures** — air, water, soil, forests and how to protect them;
- **beyond Earth** — stars, constellations and the solar system.

In every chapter, keep using the scientific method: observe, ask, guess, test, conclude, and ask again.

Science is a team effort spread across centuries and countries. Ancient Indian thinkers like **Aryabhata** (born 476 CE) explained that the Earth spins on its axis; **Sushruta** described surgical methods; modern scientists like **Jagadish Chandra Bose** studied plants' responses using instruments he designed; **Janaki Ammal** bred improved varieties of sugarcane. One day your question might add a page to this story.

Imagine a Sunday morning walk with your class through a park in Pune, or along a field edge near your village. Your teacher hands everyone a notebook and gives one simple job: **write down every different living thing you see.** In ten minutes you have a neem tree, a mango tree, a tulsi plant, grass, a money plant climbing a wall, a pumpkin vine sprawling on the ground, sparrows, a squirrel, ants, a cow, butterflies and a frog by a puddle.

Now comes the surprise question: *how many different kinds of living things are there on Earth?* Scientists have named roughly 1.5 to 2 million species, and they think millions more are still unnamed. India alone is home to a huge share of them — from snow leopards in Ladakh to mangrove crabs in the Sundarbans.

This enormous variety of life is called **biodiversity** (bio = life, diversity = variety). With so much variety, how do scientists keep track? The same way you sort a messy cupboard: by **grouping things that share features**. In this chapter we will:

- observe plants closely — their height, stems, leaves, roots and seeds;
- discover hidden links, such as between leaf veins and roots;
- look at animals and how their bodies suit the places they live;
- understand habitats and adaptations;
- learn why protecting diversity is everyone's job.

> **Did you know?** The Western Ghats and the Himalaya are counted among the world's biodiversity hotspots — places very rich in species found nowhere else.

Imagine a ship's captain in the middle of the Indian Ocean. There is no road sign, no shop, no tree — only water in every direction. Yet the captain can radio a rescue team and say *exactly* where the ship is. How?

On land we describe places using **landmarks**: "Turn left after the temple, our house is opposite the banyan tree." That works in a village or a neighbourhood. But for the whole Earth we need something better — a system that works in deserts, oceans and snowfields, a system everybody in the world agrees on.

In this chapter you will learn:

- what a **map** is and its three key components — **distance, direction and symbols**;
- what a **globe** is and why it is the truest model of the Earth;
- the **imaginary lines** we draw on the globe — the **Equator**, **latitudes** (parallels) and **longitudes** (meridians);
- how these lines form a **grid** that gives every place an "address";
- how longitudes help us understand **time** — and why India has one standard time, **IST**.

> Did you know? Long before satellites, Indian sailors crossing the Arabian Sea measured how high the Pole Star stood above the horizon to judge how far north they had sailed.

In 1972 astronauts on their way to the Moon turned their camera back towards home and took a photograph now called the *Blue Marble*. What did they see? Not brown and green, mostly — but **blue**. Water covers about **71%** of our planet's surface; land covers only about **29%**.

That is why some people joke that our planet should be called *Ocean*, not *Earth*.

In this chapter we travel around this blue marble. We will learn:

- how land and water are distributed over the Earth;
- the words geographers use for different water bodies;
- the five **oceans** and some of their features;
- the seven **continents** and what makes each special;
- how ocean water **moves** — waves, tides and currents;
- why oceans matter to life, weather, trade and India, and how we are exploring them.

Remember the hemispheres from Chapter 1? Here is a surprise: the **Northern Hemisphere** has much more land than the Southern. It is sometimes called the *Land Hemisphere*, while the Southern is called the *Water Hemisphere*.

History is not just a list of dates to memorise. It helps us:

- **understand who we are** — our languages, foods, festivals and ideas all have long histories;
- **learn from the past** — successes and mistakes;
- **appreciate our heritage** — monuments, texts, art and traditions;
- **think like a detective** — question evidence, compare sources, avoid jumping to conclusions.

Historians also **change their minds** when new evidence appears — a newly found inscription or a new dating method can rewrite a chapter. That is not weakness; it is honesty. When you read that "historians are not sure" or "estimates vary", that is the detective being careful.

**Try it:** Your own family is a source library! Old photographs, ration cards, wedding invitations, a grandparent's memories (oral history) — together they let you build your family timeline.

Open the **Constitution of India** to its very first article. It begins: *"India, that is Bharat, shall be a Union of States."* Two names, side by side, in the most important document of our Republic.

Why two? And where did these names come from?

A country's names are like the layers in an archaeological site. Each layer was laid down by different people at different times — by the land's own poets and sages, by neighbours across the mountains, by traders arriving by sea. By peeling back these layers, we discover how people inside and outside saw this land.

In this chapter we explore:

- what we mean by the **Indian subcontinent**;
- names used **within** India — *Jambudvipa*, *Bharata*, *Bharatavarsha*, and others;
- names for **regions** inside the land;
- names given by **outsiders** — Persians, Greeks, Chinese — such as *Hindu*, *Indoi*, *Yintu*, *Hindustan*;
- how these names tell a story of **unity in a vast land**.

Look at a map of Asia. A great triangle of land juts south into the Indian Ocean, separated from the rest of Asia by the mighty **Himalayas** and other mountain ranges in the north. Because it is so large and so clearly marked off, geographers call it a **subcontinent** — a large part of a continent with its own distinct identity.

Today the Indian subcontinent includes several countries: **India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, Sri Lanka** and, in many definitions, the **Maldives**. For most of history, these modern borders did not exist; people, goods and ideas moved freely across this region.

Boundaries of the subcontinent:

- **North**: the Himalayas;
- **West**: the Arabian Sea and mountain ranges like the Hindu Kush and Sulaiman ranges;
- **East**: the Bay of Bengal and the hills of the North-East;
- **South**: the Indian Ocean.

These natural boundaries helped give the land a shared character, even though inside it there were many kingdoms, languages and ways of life. Passes in the north-west mountains, such as the **Khyber** and **Bolan**, and the long coasts were the doors through which traders, travellers and armies came and went.

In the 1920s, archaeologists digging into large mounds at **Harappa** (on the Ravi river, in today's Pakistani Punjab) and **Mohenjo-daro** (on the Indus, in Sindh) found something astonishing: streets laid out in a grid, houses of baked bricks of standard size, bathrooms, and covered drains running along the streets. These cities were about **4,500 years old** — as old as the great pyramids of Egypt.

This was the discovery of the **Harappan civilisation**, named after the first excavated site. Because its cities spread along the **Indus** and the now-dry **Sarasvati** (often identified with the Ghaggar-Hakra river system), many scholars also call it the **Indus civilisation** or **Indus-Sarasvati civilisation**.

In this chapter you will learn:

- what a **civilisation** is;
- how the Harappan cities were found, and where and when the Harappans lived;
- how they **planned cities** and managed **water**;
- their **farming, crafts, daily life and trade**;
- their **seals and script**, and how they may have been governed;
- and the mystery of how their cities **declined** — and what continued.

In the previous chapters we saw how farming villages like **Mehrgarh** appeared. Over thousands of years, some villages grew into towns, and towns into cities. When a society develops features like these, historians call it a **civilisation**:

- **cities** with planned layouts and large buildings;
- a system of **government** or administration to organise people and resources;
- **specialised crafts** — potters, metal workers, bead-makers, weavers;
- **trade**, both nearby and long-distance;
- some form of **writing** or record-keeping;
- shared **art, beliefs and customs**;
- a **surplus** of food produced by farmers to feed city dwellers.

The world's early civilisations grew up near rivers — in **Mesopotamia** (Tigris–Euphrates), **Egypt** (Nile), **China** (Yellow River) and the **Indian subcontinent** (Indus and Sarasvati). Of these, the Harappan civilisation covered the **largest area**.

**Dholavira**, on Khadir island in the Rann of Kutch, lay in a dry region with little rain. Its people became master water managers. They:

- built **dams** across two seasonal streams (the Mansar and Manhar) to divert water;
- cut huge **reservoirs** into rock and lined them with stone, storing rainwater for the dry months;
- connected them with channels and drains.

Dholavira also gave us a famous find: a **'signboard'** of ten large Harappan signs made of inlaid white material, probably once fixed above a gateway. It is among the longest Harappan inscriptions ever found — but we still cannot read it!

This care for water — reservoirs, wells, stepped tanks — runs through Indian history, from Harappan tanks to medieval stepwells like Rani ki Vav in Patan.

Have you seen an old banyan tree? Its branches send down aerial roots that reach the ground and become new trunks. After centuries, one banyan can look like a small forest — yet it is one living tree. The great banyan in the Acharya Jagadish Chandra Bose Botanic Garden near Kolkata covers an area larger than a football field.

Indian culture is often compared to such a banyan. It has many roots and many branches — the **Vedas**, the **Upanishads**, schools of **philosophy**, **Buddhism**, **Jainism**, and countless **folk and tribal traditions** — which have grown, mixed and supported one another for thousands of years.

In this chapter we explore some of these roots:

1. The Vedas and the Vedic people's ideas;
2. The Upanishads and the great questions they asked;
3. Schools of philosophy;
4. Buddhism and the teachings of the Buddha;
5. Jainism and the teachings of Mahavira;
6. Folk and tribal traditions;
7. The shared values that run through them all.

Remember: we are studying ideas and history, not deciding which belief is 'right'. Understanding the roots helps us respect the whole tree.

Think back to the last time you were ill. Who brought you water? Who called the doctor? Who reminded you to take your medicine, made you khichdi, or told you a story to pass the time? Perhaps a parent, a grandparent, an aunt, an older sibling. Maybe a neighbour dropped in with fruit, and your classmates sent you homework notes.

None of us lives alone. From the day we are born we are held in **circles of care**: first the **family**, then the wider **community** — neighbours, friends, school, the village or the colony, the town.

In this chapter we explore:

- what a family is and the different **types of families**;
- how to map your own family with a **family tree**;
- the **roles and values** we learn in families;
- what a **community** is and how it supports us;
- how families and communities **work together** — in festivals, in crises and in daily life;
- how families are **changing**, and the idea that the **whole world is one family**.

A **community** is a larger group of people who are linked by something in common — where they live, their work, their language, their beliefs, or their interests — and who **support each other**.

Examples:

- the people of a **village** or a **housing colony**;
- a **school** community of students, teachers and staff;
- communities of **farmers, fishers, weavers** or **artisans**;
- **religious** or **language** communities;
- communities of interest — a cricket club, a music group, an online learning group.

You belong to **many communities at once**.

**How communities help**

- **Celebrations** — festivals, weddings and fairs are organised together. A Durga Puja pandal, a Ganeshotsav mandal, a village *mela* need everyone's help.
- **Shared resources** — village ponds, wells, grazing lands, places of worship, gurudwara *langars* (community kitchens that feed anyone, free).
- **Help in hard times** — neighbours rush to help in floods, fires or illness. During the COVID-19 pandemic, many communities set up food kitchens and helped elderly neighbours get groceries and medicines.
- **Rules and customs** that help people live together — like taking turns for water or keeping streets clean.
